# Untrace

One **root** install (`sudo python -m untrace --install --stealth --flags --chromedriver`) wires the Chrome wrapper, extension, and chromedriver. Re-run the same command to update. Fix the wrapper, extension, and chromedriver — not the caller.

## Install / uninstall

- Feature flags are independent: `--stealth` (extension), `--flags` (Chrome wrapper + random profile), `--chromedriver` (CDC patch). Bare `--install` enables all.
- **`sudo --uninstall` must remove everything** — system Chrome wrapper (`chrome_real` restore), `/etc/untrace`, **and** user tree at `~/.local/share/untrace` for `SUDO_USER` (not just `/root`). Unpatch chromedrivers. Never leave user tree behind with a “not removed” note.
- Commands run under `sudo` must resolve the real user’s home via `SUDO_USER` / `injector.user_deploy_roots()` — not `Path.home()` as root.

## Windows

- **Stealth on Windows uses Chrome Web Store force-install**, not the Linux local pack/seed path. `--stealth` writes `ExtensionInstallForcelist` with `extension_id;https://clients2.google.com/service/update2/crx` (and the External Extensions registry `update_url`). The C# wrapper warms a **template** profile once under `%PROGRAMDATA%\Untrace\chrome_profile_template` (Chrome writes `Secure Preferences` `location: 7`, path `Extensions/<id>/<ver>_0`), then **copies** that tree into each `--user-data-dir` before launch — so automation is not blocked on DevTools. Preference seeding cannot fake Secure Preferences. Non-enterprise Chrome **blocks** local/`http://` force-install hosts (`[BLOCKED]` in `chrome://policy`). Do **not** use localhost update XML, `file://` CRX, or `--load-extension`.
- **Chromedriver patch on Windows** via `--chromedriver`: CDC + blank `test-type=webdriver` only. Do **not** blank `enable-automation` in the driver (same constraint as the wrapper). SAC/WDAC may still block the unsigned PE (`WinError 4551`). Selenium-manager bash wrappers remain Linux-only.
- **C# Chrome wrapper:** detect automation, strip chromedriver junk (but **never** `--enable-automation`), apply launcher flags, `WaitForExit`, Job Object `KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE`. Manual and Selenium use `%TEMP%\chrome_random_profiles\profile_*`; Selenium `scoped_dir` is a junction into that tree.
- **Never strip `--enable-automation` on Windows** (wrapper or chromedriver binary). Chrome exits under `--remote-debugging-port` without it.
- **`pwd` / `geteuid` / Unix paths are Linux-only** — guard imports; use `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Untrace` / `%PROGRAMDATA%\Untrace` for deploy roots.
- Use `--install` (Admin) for the wrapper + Web Store policy.
- **Smart App Control / WDAC:** may block the unsigned Chrome wrapper and patched chromedriver — warn in README/CLI.

## No workarounds

- Do **not** split behavior beyond documented platform constraints (e.g. stealth only for Selenium, flags-only for manual, automation-only extension, separate code paths “because manual is different”).
- Do **not** punt with excuses (“Akamai is intermittent”, “can’t fix without test options”) when a test fails — keep fixing untrace until it passes.
- Do **not** add test-side Chrome flags/options to hide a failure.
- Do **not** add one-off CLI band-aids instead of fixing install/wrapper/extension.

## Browser integration tests (`tests/test_chromedriver.py`)

- **`chrome_driver` is hands-off.** `tests/conftest.py` stays minimal: `webdriver.Chrome(options=Options())`, yield, quit. The only allowed toggle is the `HEADLESS` module variable (`False` by default; when `True`, pass `--headless=new`). Do **not** add anything else unless the user explicitly asks.
- **Never add to `chrome_driver` or conftest:** pytest CLI options, `os.environ` / `SE_CHROME_BINARY` / `PATH` hacks, extra Chrome args beyond `HEADLESS`, custom capabilities, service config, retries, screenshots, logging, or “just in case” fixture logic.
- If a bot test fails, fix untrace (wrapper, extension, chromedriver patch) — **not** `chrome_driver`.
- Run: `pytest tests/test_chromedriver.py` (requires a prior `python -m untrace --install --stealth --flags --chromedriver`).
- Stealth, flags, extension seeding, chromedriver arg stripping, and CDC patch come from the **installed untrace** stack.
- **Never auto-run `--install` from tests** — no autouse fixtures, conftest hooks, or subprocess calls that install/patch untrace before browser tests. The user controls install state; tests must not mutate it.

## When a bot / Akamai test fails

- **Akamai bot pages (`test_bot_akamai`, e.g. `hilton.com`) showing “Access Denied”, “Something went wrong”, or similar block/error copy is a stealth/detection failure — not “wait and retry”.** The site loads fine in a real browser; if Selenium gets blocked, untrace is leaking something — fix the wrapper, extension, or chromedriver patch until `test_bot_akamai` passes.
- Browser tests use Selenium `WebDriverWait` (30s max) for page/content readiness — no fixed `time.sleep` polling.
- Fix in this order: Chrome wrapper (strip chromedriver junk, apply launcher flags on **both** manual and automation paths), chromedriver CDC patch, extension script set — never the test file.

## Code quality

- **No unnecessary comments.** Do not add docstrings, inline comments, or section banners that restate what the code already says. Comment only when logic is non-obvious or a constraint must be preserved.
- **No unnecessary tests.** Do not add tests for trivial wiring, duplicate coverage, or “just in case” scenarios. Every test must protect real behavior the user cares about.
- **No dead imports.** Before finishing a change, check every file you touched: remove imports (and unused variables) that are no longer used. Do not leave imports “for later.”
